# Line endings, settled once.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout writes CRLF into the working tree while
# the repository holds LF, and git reports whole files as rewritten when
# nothing in them changed. It has happened three times in this repo, most
# recently as "324 insertions, 324 deletions" across seven files that were
# byte-identical in every way that matters.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: decide for yourself which files are text, and
# store and check out those with LF everywhere, Windows included. Modern
# editors handle LF files fine, and it is what the repository already
# contains, so nothing has to be converted on the way in.
#
# Run once after adding this file, to tell git the working tree is already
# what this says it should be:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git status          # should show only .gitattributes
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts and Windows scripts must keep their native endings, because
# the interpreter cares. A .sh with CRLF fails with a confusing
# "bad interpreter" error; a .bat with LF misbehaves quietly.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Binaries git should never look inside or convert.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
